# Idea Catcher API: the recipe calls, nothing else (VS Code extension "REST Client", id humao.rest-client).
# The same calls as the curl recipe in docs/idea-catcher-first-local-test.md ("Recipe: test the local ic data").
# For all the endpoints, with their error cases, use tests/manual/catcher-api.http.
#
# Before you send anything:
#   1.  uv run catcher testdata reset                                           # No llm or YT Api calls
#   2.  docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml up --build    # (wait until the worker is healthy)
# When you are done:
#   3.  docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml down -v       # (also deletes the scratch database)
#
# ALTERNATIVE: test with the REAL LLM and the REAL YouTube instead (THIS COSTS MONEY). Use these steps INSTEAD of
# the three above; the requests below are the same, but see request 1b / 5 to start SMALL with a limit.
#
#   1.  uv run catcher testdata reset --fresh-llm-and-youtube    # tmp/ic WITHOUT the saved LLM replies and YT facts
#   2.  docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.live-test.yaml up --build     # (wait until the worker is healthy)
#   3.  docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.live-test.yaml down -v        # (when you are done)
#
#   Needs, in your .env (the live stack passes it to the worker, the local-test stack does not). Its path is
#   .env in the repository root, so ../../.env from this file (git-ignored; .env.example next to it shows the names):
#     OPENAI_API_KEY and the OPENAI_MODEL_* values (4 AI chats, 1 web clip, 2 YouTube clips, 8 Gemini summaries
#     go to OpenAI: your key and its budget cap apply), FREELLMAPI_URL and FREELLMAPI_API_KEY (28 notes go to
#     FreeLLMApi: free, but the address must be reachable FROM the container, not localhost), and YouTube is ON
#     (2 real requests, 2 minutes or more apart). Check FreeLLMApi first, from inside the stack:
#       docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.live-test.yaml exec worker curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' $FREELLMAPI_URL/models
#     (200 or 401 = reachable; 000 = not reachable). Start small: {"limit": 3} in request 1b or 5.
#   Still safe: the GitHub token and the remotes are blanked, so nothing is pulled or pushed; the chained publish
#   only commits in tmp/ic. A later `uv run catcher testdata reset` (no flag) puts the saved replies back.
#   If the log says "freellmapi unreachable ... 127.0.0.1:1" you started compose.local-test.yaml, which points the
#   LLM at a dead address on purpose (no key, no network): restart with the live file above.
#
# Send the requests in order. The two keys are the public test keys of compose.local-test.yaml and compose.live-test.yaml.
#
# THE CHAIN: only ideas.pull is scheduled (and no schedule is on in this stack). Every other job is queued by the
# job before it: pipeline.run -> (llm.reason / youtube.fetch per document) -> pipeline.publish -> another
# pipeline.run while files are left in the inbox. So after request 3, 4 or 5 you also get a pipeline.publish job
# (list it with 11a to 11e). This stack sets CHAIN_PUBLISH_PUSH=false: the publish only COMMITS in tmp/ic, it
# never pulls or pushes. A run with "limit" is one run: its publish queues no follow-up run.

@baseUrl = http://127.0.0.1:8000
@key = local-test-key-not-a-secret-0001

### 1. Is the stack ready? 200 with {"api":"ok","database":"ok","worker":"running"}
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### 1b. SIMULATE THE SCHEDULE: start a cycle of the chain with a pull (202, or 200 "existing": true)
# This is exactly what the hourly schedule does (no schedule is on in this stack): it queues one ideas.pull job
# through the same function (start_pull, dedupe key chain:pull). When the pull ends, the worker queues the rest by
# itself: pipeline.run -> llm.reason / youtube.fetch per document -> pipeline.publish -> another run while files
# are left. Watch it unfold in the job lists (6a to 12e) and the items (14a).
# The test repos have no remote, so the pull does nothing ("pulled": false) and the cycle works on the files
# already in tmp/ic's inbox. Use this INSTEAD of request 3, 4 or 5 to see the chain from the start: those start
# at the run. A pull that is already queued or running answers 200 with the same job_id (nothing new is queued).
# Careful: without a limit the run uses the normal profiles on every document. With the saved replies in tmp/ic (compose.local-test.yaml) that is free,
# like request 4; in the live stack (compose.live-test.yaml) it calls the real LLM and YouTube and COSTS MONEY.
# To try it SMALL (do this in the live stack): put {"limit": 3} in the body, as in request 5. The first run takes at
# most 3 documents and is the only run of the cycle: its publish queues no follow-up run, the other documents wait.
# Without a limit the run takes the whole inbox, and so do its follow-up runs.
# @name startPull
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/pull
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

#{"limit": 3}
{}

### 2a. OPTIONAL: what would a run do? A read-only preview job; it processes nothing (202, then 2b)
# What happens when you send this request:
#   1. The API receives it. The API does no work itself: it only writes ONE row in the Postgres table "jobs"
#      (type pipeline.preview, status "queued") and answers 202 with the job_id.
#   2. The worker (a separate container) finds that queued row and picks it up. It sets the status to "running",
#      reads the files in tmp/ic to work out what a real run would do, and then updates the SAME row to
#      "succeeded", with the report in its "result" column.
#   3. Request 2b reads that row back from Postgres.
# Nothing else is written: no job_items, no job_events, the YouTube gate is untouched and no file in tmp/ic
# changes. The row stays until the database is deleted (down -v). Sending 2a again makes another row.
# @name preview
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"dry_run": true}

### 2b. The preview job: status "succeeded" after a few seconds, then result.report.counts
# This call only READS from Postgres and writes nothing. The API looks up the one row of the preview job
# in the table "jobs" (by the job_id from 2a) and returns its properties: status, parameters, timings,
# an error if there was one, and "result", the report the worker saved there.
# The documents are in result.report.names.items: one entry per document in the inbox, with its name,
# doc_id, doc_class, a status (for example would_publish) and llm_saved. They come from the saved report,
# not from document records: a preview creates no job_items rows. "queued" or "running" in the status
# means the worker is not done yet: send 2b again.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs/{{preview.response.body.$.job_id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 3. QUEUE THE DATA without the LLM and without YouTube: one pipeline.run job with the fake profile (202)
# This is the same call as 2a (POST /api/v1/pipeline/runs), but WITHOUT "dry_run": so it is a real run that
# PROCESSES the data. The API again only writes one "queued" row in the table "jobs" (type pipeline.run) and
# answers 202. The worker then picks it up and does the work: it stages every document of the inbox, creates
# the document records (job_items) and one llm.reason job per document, and it writes the pages in
# tmp/ic/epiaku-docs. The result is saved in the job row; read it with request 13.
# Still no LLM and no YouTube: "profile": "fake" makes the LLM step use the fake backend (no model is
# called), YouTube is switched off in this stack and the test data holds the saved YouTube facts.
# @name startRun
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"profile": "fake"}

### 3b. Send the same request again right away: 200, "existing": true, the SAME job_id (no second run is queued)
# Purpose: to see that the API does not queue a second run while one is still waiting or running.
# What it means: you start a run that is already queued or running. The API finds that run and answers
#   200 (not 202) with "existing": true and the job_id of the FIRST run (the same id as in request 3).
#   Nothing new is written in Postgres: no second row in the table "jobs". Any options in the body are ignored.
# Why: two starts should not process the same inbox twice. It protects against a double click, two people or
#   devices starting a run, and the API starting a run while a scheduled run is already waiting.
# Timing: the run takes only a few seconds on the test data, so send 3b right after 3. If the run has already
#   finished, nothing is waiting any more: 3b then starts a NEW run (202, a new job_id). That run finds an
#   empty inbox and processes nothing.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"profile": "fake"}

### 4. ALTERNATIVE to 3: the same run with the NORMAL profiles instead of the fake one (202)
# Use this INSTEAD of request 3, not after it: the inbox can only be processed once. After 3 has finished the
# inbox is empty, so request 4 would process nothing; while 3 is still queued or running, request 4 answers
# like 3b with the job of 3 (the fake profile).
# To use request 4: reset the test data and restart the docker compose 
#
# Press `Ctrl-C` in the terminal where the docker compose runs
# docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml down -v
#
# 1. fresh test repos in tmp/ic (deletes tmp/ic first: see the note below)
# uv run catcher testdata reset
# 
# 2. build and start the whole stack: database, migrations, worker, API
# docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml up --build
#
# Why down -v and up: the reset deletes and recreates tmp/ic, and a running worker keeps looking at the old,
# deleted folder (an empty inbox). Starting the stack again makes it see the new files.
# The body is empty ({}): no "profile", so every document uses the profile it normally gets: notes use
# freellmapi, AI chats and web clips use openai (clippings), YouTube clips use openai (youtube).
#
# Still no LLM call and no YouTube call: the worker first looks for a saved reply in tmp/ic/idea-bucket/llm
# (same task, profile and document text) and finds one for every document, so no model is asked and no key is
# needed; YouTube is switched off in this stack and the saved facts are in tmp/ic/idea-bucket/facts.
# Checked with the preview (2a, which also uses the normal profiles): 43 documents would_publish, all with
# llm_saved true. If a document had no saved reply, the call would go to a dummy address that does not
# answer (nothing leaves your machine) and that document would be deferred.
#
# What differs from 3: after the run the items (request 14a) show their normal profile (notes, clippings or
# youtube) and the backend and model of the saved reply (freellmapi or openai), not "fake".
# @name startRunNormal
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### 5. ALTERNATIVE to 3 and 4: a LIVE run with the real LLM and the real YouTube (202). THIS COSTS MONEY
# The same call as request 4 (an empty body {}: every document uses its normal profile), but on test data
# WITHOUT the saved LLM replies and YouTube facts, so the worker really asks the LLMs and YouTube.
# Use it INSTEAD of 3 and 4: the inbox can only be processed once.
# What it calls: 28 notes go to FreeLLMApi (free, but it must be reachable from the container); 15 documents go
# to OpenAI (4 AI chats, 1 web clip, 2 YouTube clips, 8 Gemini summaries: your OpenAI key and budget cap apply);
# the 2 YouTube clips make 2 real YouTube requests, paced by the gate (2 minutes or more between two), so the
# run takes several minutes. The 8 Gemini summaries make no YouTube request.
# Set it up, in this order:
#   1. Stop the running stack: Ctrl-C, then
#        docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.local-test.yaml down -v
#   2. Reset the test data WITHOUT the saved replies and facts (they are left out of the copy):
#        uv run catcher testdata reset --fresh-llm-and-youtube
#   3. Start the live stack. compose.live-test.yaml passes the LLM settings of your .env to the worker, turns
#      YouTube on, and blanks the GitHub token, the remotes and every schedule (nothing can push or run by itself):
#        docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.live-test.yaml up --build
#   4. Look first with the preview (request 2a). It calls nothing and should now say would_call_llm (and
#      would_fetch for the YouTube clips) instead of would_publish. Then send this request.
# To try it small first, put {"limit": 3} in the body: only 3 documents are processed (the others stay in the
# inbox for a later run) and only those calls are paid.
# That is one run: the chained publish after it only commits here and queues no follow-up run (follow_up false),
# so the other documents wait for a run of their own.
# After it: the items (request 14a) show the real backend and model, and the replies and facts the run fetched
# are saved in tmp/ic/idea-bucket/llm and facts. uv run catcher testdata reset (without the flag) puts the
# committed saved ones back.
# @name startRunLive
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/pipeline/runs
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"limit": 3}

# ====================================================================================================
# JOBS: ALL TYPES TOGETHER (6a to 6d)
# Total per status, every job type counted: pipeline.run, llm.reason, youtube.fetch, pipeline.publish, pipeline.preview.
# ====================================================================================================

### 6a. HOW MANY JOBS? Read "total" in each answer. Jobs waiting in the queue:
#GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=queued&limit=1
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 6b. Jobs running now:
#GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=running&limit=1
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 6c. Jobs finished: about 45 on the test data (the run + one llm.reason job per document, 43, + the chained publish)
#GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=succeeded&limit=1
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 6d. Jobs failed: 0
#GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=failed&limit=1
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

# ====================================================================================================
# JOBS PER TYPE (7 to 12)
# Each type below has 5 requests: the total of the type, then the total per status.
# ====================================================================================================

# Count one type: add &type=<type> to any call of 6a to 6d. Requests 7 to 12 do it for each type.
# The types (the worker's registry): pipeline.run, pipeline.preview, llm.reason, youtube.fetch,
# pipeline.publish (pushes the pages) and ideas.pull (pulls the ideas repository).
# After request 5 with {"limit": 3} you expect: 1 pipeline.run, 3 llm.reason and 1 pipeline.publish (plus 1
# youtube.fetch per YouTube clip among them, plus 1 pipeline.preview if you sent 2a). Read "total" in each answer.
# The first call lists ALL jobs of the type, whatever the status; the next four split it by status.

# ====================================================================================================
# 7. JOBS OF TYPE llm.reason  (total, then queued / running / succeeded / failed)
# What: makes the page of ONE staged document (asks the LLM) and records the outcome on its item.
# Purpose: the actual work per document. Queued by pipeline.run, or by youtube.fetch for a YouTube clip.
# ====================================================================================================

### 7a. llm.reason: one per document: asks the LLM and publishes the page
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=llm.reason
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 7b. llm.reason, queued
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=llm.reason&status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 7c. llm.reason, running
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=llm.reason&status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 7d. llm.reason, succeeded
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=llm.reason&status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 7e. llm.reason, failed
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=llm.reason&status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

# ====================================================================================================
# 8. JOBS OF TYPE youtube.fetch  (total, then queued / running / succeeded / failed)
# What: gets the facts (transcript) of ONE staged YouTube clip, paced by the YouTube gate, then queues its llm.reason.
# Purpose: the YouTube step before the LLM step. Only clips need it; the saved facts make it unnecessary in tests.
# ====================================================================================================

### 8a. youtube.fetch: one per YouTube clip: fetches the transcript, paced by the YouTube gate
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=youtube.fetch
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 8b. youtube.fetch, queued
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=youtube.fetch&status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 8c. youtube.fetch, running
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=youtube.fetch&status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 8d. youtube.fetch, succeeded
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=youtube.fetch&status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 8e. youtube.fetch, failed
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=youtube.fetch&status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

# ====================================================================================================
# 9. JOBS OF TYPE pipeline.run  (total, then queued / running / succeeded / failed)
# What: stages every document of the inbox, creates the item records and queues one job per document.
# Purpose: the start of the whole pipeline. One job per request 3, 4 or 5.
# ====================================================================================================

### 9a. pipeline.run: the run itself: one per request 3, 4 or 5
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.run
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 9b. pipeline.run, queued
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.run&status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 9c. pipeline.run, running
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.run&status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 9d. pipeline.run, succeeded
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.run&status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 9e. pipeline.run, failed
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.run&status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

# ====================================================================================================
# 10. JOBS OF TYPE pipeline.preview  (total, then queued / running / succeeded / failed)
# What: works out what a run would do now and saves the report in the job. Changes nothing.
# Purpose: look before you run (and before a paid live run). One job per request 2a.
# ====================================================================================================

### 10a. pipeline.preview: the read-only preview of request 2a
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.preview
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 10b. pipeline.preview, queued
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.preview&status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 10c. pipeline.preview, running
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.preview&status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 10d. pipeline.preview, succeeded
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.preview&status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 10e. pipeline.preview, failed
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.preview&status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

# ====================================================================================================
# 11. JOBS OF TYPE pipeline.publish  (total, then queued / running / succeeded / failed)
# What: commits the managed folders of both repos, then pulls (rebase) and pushes.
# Purpose: gets the generated pages into git. Queued by the chain after a run (params pull/push false in this
# stack: a local commit only, follow_up false after a run with "limit"), or by hand. After request 3, 4 or 5
# the total is 1 here. This only LISTS them: it starts nothing.
# ====================================================================================================

### 11a. pipeline.publish
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.publish
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 11b. pipeline.publish, queued
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.publish&status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 11c. pipeline.publish, running
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.publish&status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 11d. pipeline.publish, succeeded
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.publish&status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 11e. pipeline.publish, failed
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=pipeline.publish&status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

# ====================================================================================================
# 12. JOBS OF TYPE ideas.pull  (total, then queued / running / succeeded / failed)
# What: commits the worker's folders locally, then pulls (rebase) the ideas repository.
# Purpose: brings in new ideas. Queued by a schedule or the CLI, never by this recipe, so the totals are
# normally 0 here. This only LISTS them: it starts nothing.
# ====================================================================================================

### 12a. ideas.pull
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=ideas.pull
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 12b. ideas.pull, queued
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=ideas.pull&status=queued
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 12c. ideas.pull, running
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=ideas.pull&status=running
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 12d. ideas.pull, succeeded
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=ideas.pull&status=succeeded
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 12e. ideas.pull, failed
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs?type=ideas.pull&status=failed
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 13. This run: status, result with the counts, item_counts per class and status
# This reads the run of request 3. If you sent request 4 instead of 3, change startRun to startRunNormal in the
# line below; if you sent request 5, change it to startRunLive.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/jobs/{{startRun.response.body.$.job_id}}
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 14a. The documents: "total" is 43 on the test data
# Read "total": it is the number of ALL matching documents. "items" holds only ONE page of them.
# "limit" is the page size: how many documents the answer holds (limit=1 shows 1, to keep the answer short).
# "offset" is where the page starts: how many documents to SKIP from the start of the list, which is sorted
# newest first. offset=0 starts at the first (newest) document, offset=1 skips 1 and shows the second one,
# and offset=42 shows the 43rd, the oldest. With limit=5 and offset=5 you get documents 6 to 10.
# To see more at once, raise the limit (limit=50 shows all 43; the maximum is 200). To walk through them one
# by one, keep limit=1 and send offset=0, 1, 2, and so on.
# (This "limit" is the page size of a list. It is not the "limit" of request 5: there it is an option of the
# run, how many documents to process.)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/items?limit=1&offset=0
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}

### 14b. The documents that are published: "total" is 43 when the run has finished
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/items?status=published&limit=1
Authorization: Bearer {{key}}
